        ) : (
          <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none prose-headings:text-gray-900 prose-p:text-gray-600 prose-li:text-gray-600">
            <h2>What Is CE Certification?</h2>
            <p>
              CE marking (Conformit&eacute; Europ&eacute;enne) indicates that a product meets EU health, safety, and
              environmental requirements. For inflatable watercraft, the relevant regulatory framework depends on the
              product category.
            </p>

            <h2>Applicable Directives</h2>

            <h3>Recreational Craft Directive (2013/53/EU)</h3>
            <p>
              The primary directive for most inflatable boats, SUPs, and kayaks. It applies to recreational craft from
              2.5 m to 24 m hull length, partially completed boats (for OEM supply), and personal watercraft.
            </p>

            <h3>Personal Protective Equipment Regulation (2016/425)</h3>
            <p>
              Life jackets, buoyancy aids, and safety equipment supplied with watercraft must meet PPE Regulation
              requirements, including type examination (Module B) by a notified body and production quality assurance
              (Module D or E).
            </p>

            <h2>The Certification Process</h2>
            <ol>
              <li>Product classification: Determine craft design category (A – D per ISO 8666)</li>
              <li>
                Applicable standards: EN ISO 6185 (Parts 1 – 4), EN ISO 10087 (HIN), EN ISO 14945 (builder's plate)
              </li>
              <li>
                Technical file preparation: Design drawings, Bill of Materials, material certificates, risk assessment
              </li>
              <li>Type testing: Buoyancy, stability, load capacity, material testing, seam strength, air retention</li>
              <li>Factory production control: Notified body audit of manufacturing processes</li>
              <li>Declaration of Conformity: Manufacturer signs DoC, affixes CE marking</li>
            </ol>

            <h2>Notified Bodies for Inflatable Watercraft</h2>
            <p>
              Common NBs include Bureau Veritas (France), DNV GL (Germany), RINA (Italy), T&Uuml;V Rheinland / T&Uuml;V
              S&Uuml;D (Germany).
            </p>

            <h2>Timeline and Costs</h2>
            <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
              <thead>
                <tr class="bg-gray-100">
                  <th class="p-3 text-left font-semibold text-gray-900 border">Phase</th>
                  <th class="p-3 text-left font-semibold text-gray-900 border">Duration</th>
                  <th class="p-3 text-left font-semibold text-gray-900 border">Typical Cost (EUR)</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr>
                  <>
                    <td class="p-3 border">Technical file preparation</td>
